    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/esbsheulrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=085252": {
      "title": "High Performance Computing Senior Engineer",
      "description": "Closing Date: 04/12/2026 High Performance Computing Senior Engineer Applications are invited for the following position: Title of Post: High Performance Computing Senior Engineer JOB SYNOPSIS The University of Limerick has embarked on the provision of a central High Performance Computing cluster and research computing supports that will be available to the UL community who require such services. The HPC Senior Engineer will provide technical leadership and ownership of the central HPC cluster to develop, optimise and deploy scalable HPC applications and workflows on HPC and cloud infrastructures using CPU and GPU architectures. Operating as part of the IT infrastructure and wider ITD teams, this role will ensure that HPC capabilities are fully available and utilised at the university. This role will be responsible for the technical management of the system, ensuring it is kept up to date, key changes and improvements are communicated and the research community is supported in using the HPC facilities and other key research computing services. Office of the Director of Information Technology Information Technology Division Contract Type: Permanent Salary Scale: €61,642 - €87,643 p.a. University of Limerick is a research-led, energetic and enterprising institution with a proud record of innovation and excellence in education and scholarships. We value creativity, curiosity and excellence in our inclusive community which is committed to social good. Our motto Eagna chun Gnímh translates as Wisdom for Action reflecting our mission to contribute to the discovery of new knowledge for a better world. Informal enquiries regarding the post may be directed to: Mr Noel O'Brien Infrastructure Manager, ITD University of Limerick Email: noel.obrien@ul.ie The closing date for receipt of applications is Friday, 4th December 2026 Applications must be completed online before 12 noon, Irish Standard Time on the closing date. Please note your application must include: A letter of introduction and a current CV indicating how you meet the criteria outlined in the Job description. A completed online application form. Are you currently legally entitled to work and reside in Ireland? If you are not currently an EEA Citizen and have stated that you are currently legally entitled to work and reside in Ireland, you MUST upload a copy of your current Visa and Work permit in the attachments section application form. Unfortunately, if the attachment, as noted, is required and not provided your application cannot be processed. Please note your current work authorisation status must allow you to fulfil the terms of the contract on offer. Please email erecruitment@ul.ie if you experience any difficulties Applications are welcome from suitably qualified candidates. Further information for applicants and application material is available online from: University of Limerick Vacancies We are committed to upholding the principles of fairness, equality, and transparency in pay, in line with the EU Pay Transparency Directive (Directive (EU) 2023/970) Many thanks for your interest in this role. Contact Person : Competition Type : Staff Contact Email : Job ID : 085252 Contact Number : Close Date : 04-Dec-2026 12:00 Further Particulars : "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/esbsheulrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=085170": {
      "title": "Open Research Manager (Library)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 12/11/2026 Open Research Manager (Library) Applications are invited for the following position: Title of Post: Open Research Manager (Library) JOB SYNOPSIS The University of Limerick Library is seeking an enthusiastic and innovative Open Research Manager to play a key role in developing strategies to make University of Limerick (UL) research available as widely as possible to an international audience, with the aim of increasing UL’s research excellence and impact. Based within the Research Services Department of the Library & Information Services Division and reporting to the Head of Library Research Services, the postholder will work across the institution to ensure that UL’s research is published in high-quality, openly accessible venues, reaches the widest possible audience, and achieves the greatest possible societal and scholarly impact. The postholder will be UL’s primary expert in open access publishing, scholarly communications, and the responsible use of research metrics. They will provide hands-on advisory, training, and advocacy work for researchers at all career stages while also contributing strategic analysis and policy engagement to support UL’s research ambitions. They will also lead and manage the institutional repository, including its operations, team, and strategic development. The role will be central to UL meeting its obligations under the National Open Research Forum (NORF) 100% Open Access target and to fulfilling the commitments of UL’s Wisdom for Action Research Strategy 2022-2027 and subsequent strategies. Office of the Provost & Deputy President Library & Information Services Division Contract Type: Permanent Salary Scale: €61,642 - €87,643 p.a. University of Limerick is a research-led, energetic and enterprising institution with a proud record of innovation and excellence in education and scholarships. We value creativity, curiosity and excellence in our inclusive community which is committed to social good. Our motto Eagna chun Gnímh translates as Wisdom for Action reflecting our mission to contribute to the discovery of new knowledge for a better world. Informal enquiries regarding the post may be directed to: Ms Ashling Hayes Head Library Research Services University of Limerick Email: ashling.hayes@ul.ie The closing date for receipt of applications is Thursday, 12th November 2026 Applications must be completed online before 12 noon, Irish Standard Time on the closing date. Please note your application must include: A letter of introduction and a current CV indicating how you meet the criteria outlined in the Job description. A completed online application form. Are you currently legally entitled to work and reside in Ireland? If you are not currently an EEA Citizen and have stated that you are currently legally entitled to work and reside in Ireland, you MUST upload a copy of your current Visa and Work permit in the attachments section application form. Unfortunately, if the attachment, as noted, is required and not provided your application cannot be processed. Please note your current work authorisation status must allow you to fulfil the terms of the contract on offer. Please email erecruitment@ul.ie if you experience any difficulties Applications are welcome from suitably qualified candidates. Further information for applicants and application material is available online from: University of Limerick Vacancies We are committed to upholding the principles of fairness, equality, and transparency in pay, in line with the EU Pay Transparency Directive (Directive (EU) 2023/970) Many thanks for your interest in this role. Contact Person : Competition Type : Staff Contact Email : Job ID : 085170 Contact Number : Close Date : 12-Nov-2026 12:00 Further Particulars : "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/esbsheulrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=085150": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Researcher (Level 1) in Composites Manufacturing",
      "description": "Closing Date: 09/11/2026 Post Doctoral Researcher (Level 1) in Composites Manufacturing Applications are invited for the following position: Title of Post: Post Doctoral Researcher (Level 1) in Composites Manufacturing JOB SYNOPSIS We are seeking a skilled, motivated Aerospace Composites R&D Engineer to support the development of advanced composite materials, lightweight structures, and innovative manufacturing technologies. The successful candidate will lead research, process development, prototype manufacturing, mechanical characterisation, and structural validation of composite components. The position will focus on automated manufacturing, thermoplastic composites, process optimisation, sustainable materials, and recycling technologies. The engineer will collaborate with multidisciplinary teams, academic institutions, and industrial partners to translate research outcomes into practical engineering solutions, thereby advancing technological innovation and industrial competitiveness. Faculty of Science & Engineering School of Engineering Contract Type: Specific Purpose Salary Scale: €47,273 - €53,925 (maximum starting salary €47,273) p.a. pro rata University of Limerick is a research-led, energetic and enterprising institution with a proud record of innovation and excellence in education and scholarships. We value creativity, curiosity and excellence in our inclusive community which is committed to social good. Our motto Eagna chun Gnímh translates as Wisdom for Action reflecting our mission to contribute to the discovery of new knowledge for a better world. Informal enquiries regarding the post may be directed to: Professor Paul Weaver Bernal Chair in Composite Materials and their Structures School of Engineering University of Limerick Email: paul.weaver@ul.ie The closing date for receipt of applications is Monday 9th November 2026 Applications must be completed online before 12 noon, Irish Standard Time on the closing date. “This is a professional training and development role and the training and development relevant to this position will be completed within the period of the contract. Post Doctoral Researchers appointed will be expected to complete the Researcher Career Development Programme.” Please note your application must include: A letter of introduction and a current CV indicating how you meet the criteria outlined in the Job description. A completed online application form. Please email erecruitment@ul.ie if you experience any difficulties Applications are welcome from suitably qualified candidates. Further information for applicants and application material is available online from: University of Limerick Vacancies We are committed to upholding the principles of fairness, equality, and transparency in pay, in line with the EU Pay Transparency Directive (Directive (EU) 2023/970) Many thanks for your interest in this role. Contact Person : Competition Type : Research Contact Email : Job ID : 085150 Contact Number : Close Date : 09-Nov-2026 12:00 Further Particulars : "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/esbsheulrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=085069": {
      "title": "Post Doctoral Researcher (Level 1) in Privacy-Preserving AI and Blockchain Trading Architectures (Re-advertisement)",
      "description": "Closing Date: 05/11/2026 Post Doctoral Researcher (Level 1) in Privacy-Preserving AI and Blockchain Trading Architectures (Re-advertisement) Applications are invited for the following position: Title of Post: Post Doctoral Researcher (Level 1) in Privacy-Preserving AI and Blockchain Trading Architectures (Re-advertisement) JOB SYNOPSIS The Post Doctoral Researcher will carry out research in the area of privacy-preserving blockchain trading architectures, with a focus on zero-knowledge proof systems (including hardware acceleration of ZKP circuits), homomorphic encryption for smart contract computation, federated blockchain data architectures, and blockchain forensics via graph analysis. The research is conducted within the EthER3 programme, funded by Lero (the SFI Research Centre for Software) in collaboration with the industry partner Horizon Globex Ireland DAC. The researcher will also contribute to the day-to-day supervision of PhD students and to knowledge transfer activities with the industry partner. University of Limerick is a research-led, energetic and enterprising institution with a proud record of innovation and excellence in education and scholarships. We value creativity, curiosity and excellence in our inclusive community which is committed to social good. Our motto Eagna chun Gnímh translates as Wisdom for Action, reflecting our mission to contribute to the discovery of new knowledge for a better world. Faculty of Science & Engineering Department of Computer Science & Information Systems Contract Type: Specific Purpose Salary Scale: €47,273 - €53,925 p.a. pro rata (maximum starting salary €47,273 p.a. pro rata) University of Limerick is a research-led, energetic and enterprising institution with a proud record of innovation and excellence in education and scholarships. We value creativity, curiosity and excellence in our inclusive community which is committed to social good. Our motto Eagna chun Gnímh translates as Wisdom for Action reflecting our mission to contribute to the discovery of new knowledge for a better world. Informal enquiries regarding the post may be directed to: Dr Andrew Le Gear Department of Computer Science & Information Systems University of Limerick Email: andrew.p.legear@ul.ie The closing date for receipt of applications is Thursday, 5th November 2026 Applications must be completed online before 12 noon, Irish Standard Time on the closing date. “This is a professional training and development role and the training and development relevant to this position will be completed within the period of the contract. Post Doctoral Researchers appointed will be expected to complete the Researcher Career Development Programme.” Please note your application must include: A letter of introduction and a current CV indicating how you meet the criteria outlined in the Job description. A completed online application form. Please email erecruitment@ul.ie if you experience any difficulties Applications are welcome from suitably qualified candidates. Further information for applicants and application material is available online from: University of Limerick Vacancies Many thanks for your interest in this role. Contact Person : Competition Type : Research Contact Email : Job ID : 085069 Contact Number : Close Date : 05-Nov-2026 12:00 Further Particulars : "
    },
    "https://my.corehr.com/pls/esbsheulrecruit/erq_jobspec_version_4.jobspec?p_id=085309": {
      "title": "Project Manager (Construction) - Buildings & Estates",
      "description": "Closing Date: 24/11/2026 Project Manager (Construction) - Buildings & Estates Applications are invited for the following position: Title of Post: Project Manager (Construction) - Buildings & Estates JOB SYNOPSIS Reporting to the Projects Officer, the appointment of the Project Manager within the Projects Team is critical in supporting the overall estates strategy which sets out a vision and plan for future development and growth of the university estate. This is a high visibility role requiring highly developed professional, strategic, operational, cost, contract, and project management skills to assist in the delivery of the University’s ongoing small capital development programme. The projects can range from small works (€50k) to large capital projects exceeding €40m in value. Office of the Director, Buildings & Estates Contract Type: Permanent Salary Scale: €61,642 - €87,643 p.a. University of Limerick is a research-led, energetic and enterprising institution with a proud record of innovation and excellence in education and scholarships. We value creativity, curiosity and excellence in our inclusive community which is committed to social good. Our motto Eagna chun Gnímh translates as Wisdom for Action reflecting our mission to contribute to the discovery of new knowledge for a better world. Informal enquiries regarding the post may be directed to: Mr Gerard Manning Acting Director Buildings & Estates Buildings & Estates Department University of Limerick Email: gerard.manning@ul.ie The closing date for receipt of applications is Tuesday, 24th November 2026 Applications must be completed online before 12 noon, Irish Standard Time on the closing date. Please note your application must include: A letter of introduction and a current CV indicating how you meet the criteria outlined in the Job description. A completed online application form. Are you currently legally entitled to work and reside in Ireland? If you are not currently an EEA Citizen 